47. Fiscal Policy: Views, Instruments and Fiscal Reforms in India
Paper: Public Finance (ECNCC303) | Unit: VI | Topic: Classical, neo-classical and modern views on fiscal policy; countercyclical and compensatory fiscal policy; limitations; integration of monetary and fiscal policies; fiscal reforms in India
Introduction
Fiscal policy is the use of government expenditure, taxation and borrowing to influence the level of economic activity, prices, growth and distribution.
Classical and neo-classical views
Classical: Say's law ensures full employment, so fiscal policy has no stabilising role; balanced budgets, minimal government; deficits crowd out private investment. Neo-classical: markets adjust, but government may correct market failures; the supply-side school (Laffer) favours tax cuts to raise incentives; monetarists (Friedman) see money as the main influence and fiscal impact as weak.
Modern (Keynesian) view
Keynes: output is determined by aggregate demand, with unemployment possible. Government spending and tax changes act through multipliers. Fiscal multiplier: 1/(1 - MPC) for spending; tax multiplier is smaller: MPC/(1 - MPC). Balanced-budget multiplier equals one. Later: new classical (rational expectations and Ricardian equivalence) challenge effectiveness; new Keynesian models restore the role with price stickiness.
Countercyclical and compensatory fiscal policy
• Countercyclical: surplus in booms and deficit in recession, via automatic stabilisers (progressive taxes, unemployment benefits) and discretionary measures.
• Compensatory fiscal policy: public spending to compensate for fall in private spending; the government as spender of last resort. Cyclically adjusted budget and the fiscal impulse measure stance.
Limitations
Time lags (recognition, decision, implementation, impact), crowding out, uncertainty about multipliers, political economy (deficit bias), debt sustainability, supply constraints in developing countries (structural inflation), leakage via imports, and Ricardian offsets.
Integration of monetary and fiscal policies
The policy mix affects output, interest rates and inflation: expansionary fiscal with tight monetary raises interest rates and crowds out; both expansionary raises demand sharply. IS-LM analysis shows the interdependence. Coordination matters: fiscal dominance (the central bank financing deficits) versus monetary independence; India's FRBM framework and RBI's inflation targeting; ending automatic monetisation (1997 ad hoc treasury bill phase-out).
Fiscal reforms in the context of India's New Economic Policy
• 1991 onwards: reduction of fiscal deficit, subsidy rationalisation, reduction of customs duties, introduction of MODVAT/VAT and then GST (2017), service tax, simplified direct taxes with a lower corporate rate, disinvestment.
• FRBM Act 2003: rules for deficit and debt targets; the NK Singh Committee (2017) recommended debt anchor.
• State-level reforms: VAT, FRBM at state level.
• Results: improved tax buoyancy and market-determined interest rates, but persistent deficits and rising debt after shocks.
Conclusion
Fiscal policy is a powerful but constrained tool; its effectiveness depends on context, credibility and coordination with monetary policy.